  "summary": "A heavy centrifugal pump built to move abrasive mixtures of liquid and solids (mine tailings, dredge spoil, mineral concentrates). Large, slow-running impellers with thick shrouds and wide passages limit wear; the wetted surfaces are replaceable liners of hard white iron or rubber held in a split outer casing. Pump-out vanes on the impeller and an expeller behind it keep slurry away from the gland. Speed is set by the V-belt ratio, so the duty can be matched without trimming the impeller.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Rotodynamic (centrifugal), radial flow",
    "configuration": "Horizontal, end suction, lined, overhung, belt-driven"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "teaching_overlays": "wear (x-ray map of typical wear zones by severity), flow",
    "liners": "Hard-metal (high-chrome white iron) volute liner with throatbush; rubber-lined versions exist for fine slurries",
    "shaft_seal": "Expeller + gland packing (centrifugal seal while running; packing for standstill)",
    "drive": "Overhead-mounted motor, 3 V-belts (ratio ~1.8:1 shown)",
    "note": "Slurry pump selection uses head/efficiency derating for solids (ANSI/HI 12.1-12.6)."
  },
